### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):                               
The dataset contained 10,001 time points of a 9-variable state vector. Assuming 
a standard sampling interval ($dt = 0.01$), the time derivatives were           
approximated using central differences. A library of potential functional terms 
was constructed, including a constant bias, linear states ($x_1 \dots x_9$), and
all possible quadratic interactions (squares $x_i^2$ and products $x_i x_j$).   
                                                                                
A sparse identification of non-linear dynamics (SINDy) algorithm was applied,   
which uses sequential thresholded least squares to identify the most significant
contributors to the dynamics of each variable. With a threshold of 0.01, it was 
found that nearly all quadratic terms are relevant in describing the evolution  
of the system. The coefficients were then rounded to three decimal places to    
provide the final governing ODEs.                                               
                                                                                
For example, the rate of change of $x_1$, denoted as $x_1[1]$, is governed by a 
combination of a bias (91.678), linear contributions from all variables, and    
non-linear interactions between almost every pair of variables. Similar         
structures were found for the other variables in the system.                    
                                                                                
### 3. Additional context (if relevant):                                        
The complexity of the equations and the inclusion of almost every possible      
quadratic term suggests that the system exhibits highly coupled non-linear      
behavior. The identified model is purely data-driven and relies on the          
assumption that the underlying dynamics are polynomial of degree up to 2. The   
consistency of the coefficient magnitudes across the variables indicates a      
strongly interconnected dynamical network.                                      
